  "worked_solution": "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{\u2032}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=5\\,730$ years, we have\n\n$$\n5\\,730=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{k}\\,\u21d2\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{5\\,730}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}\u20612/5\\,730}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.3{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.3{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}\u20612/5\\,730}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.3{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}\u20612/5\\,730} \\\\ 0.3\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}\u20612/5\\,730} \\\\ 0.3 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}\u20612/5\\,730} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.3) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}\u20612/5\\,730}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.3) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{5\\,730} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{5\\,730\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.3)}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612} \\\\ t & \u22489\\,953\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the object is approximately $9\\,953$ years old.",
